{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,230p' vendor/engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js && sed -n '1,130p' vendor/engine/crawler.js\"","aggregated_output":"/*!\nmy-3D2dge 3D v0.89.1: real 3D worlds under my-3D2dge games (three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3)\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge (MIT License)\n\nA 3D world, its physics and its camera under a my-3D2dge game. The 2D engine (engine/my-3d2dge.js) still runs the game:\nits exact fixed steps, scenes, input, pause menu, settings, touch buttons, sound, the rigs and their moves, the HUD. This\nmodule simulates and draws the world under the game's clear canvas. Everything is drawn by code: levels written as text,\ntextures pixel by pixel, characters by the 2D engine's rigs (as cards, or as 3D puppets on the same joints), props and\npickups from four shapes. No image, model or sound files.\n\nAGENTS: this header is the whole manual for the 3D side; the API card (API.md) covers the 2D side (E.Game, input, rigs,\nmoves, E.font, E.ui, E.Menu, audio). Read the code below to debug it, or to change it (it is yours to change, in any\nway you see fit): its sections start with \"/* ===\" and a number.\n\n## Host it (served: browsers load modules only over http; npx serve at the repository's root, or my-3d2dge.vercel.app)\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": { \"three\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js\", \"three/tsl\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js\" } }</script>\n<style>html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; background: #000 }</style>\n<div id=\"stage\" style=\"position:relative;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:hidden\">\n  <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%\"></canvas></div>\n<script src=\"/dist/my-3d2dge.js\"></script>                   the 2D engine, a classic script, first\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js\"></script>  this file\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"game.js\"></script>                your game, a module (const E = My3D2dge;)\nInlined into a page's own script tag instead, this file ends the page at its first closing script tag (this header has\nfour): write each with a backslash before its slash, as the build's single files do.\n\n## Mental model\n- The 2D engine's units: x east, y south, z up; 16 units = 1 metre = 1 tile; speeds in units a second; angles in\n  radians from +x toward +y (0 east, PI/2 south, -PI/2 north). The axes are left-handed (three.js's are right-handed):\n  a mesh of your own built in engine units comes out mirrored, its winding and turns flipped; place it in three.js's\n  (E.World3D.toThree(x, y, z): x, z, y over 16).\n- new E.Game({ ..., bg: null }): its canvas is clear, and the HUD draws on it over the 3D picture (r.overlay, r.text,\n  E.ui, E.font) in game pixels, r.W x r.H. res: 'ps1', expand: true as in the 2D games.\n- await E.World3D.init(game) once (a boot step), then new E.World3D(game, { level, ... }) for each level. In a scene:\n  update(dt) { your rules, then world.step(dt) } and draw(r) { world.draw(r), then your HUD }. Paused or frozen\n  (game.freeze), update doesn't run, so the world holds still and still draws. A frame between steps draws the actors\n  and the camera part way from the step before (game.alpha: even motion at 144 Hz); a pose set in onDraw is placed as set.\n- Deterministic: the world steps in the game's fixed steps; gameplay randomness comes from E.rng(seed); the camera's\n  pose is worked out in the steps. world.reset() then the same inputs give the same world.hash() on WebGPU, WebGL 2 and\n  run again. Gameplay never reads the picture or the GPU: it reads actors, props and the level.\n- The camera decides what \"up the stick\" means: move with world.camera.ground(game.input.move()).\n\n## Quick start: a complete game (collect the gems, mind the slimes)\n```js quickstart\nconst E = My3D2dge;\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input: E.World3D.INPUT, menu: { settings: E.World3D.SETTINGS } });\nconst LEVEL = { rows: [\n  '################',\n  '#t............t#',\n  '#..g.....==..g.#',\n  '#........==....#',\n  '#..s.....^^....#',\n  '#.............s#',\n  '#..g...@....g..#',\n  '#t............t#',\n  '################'],\n  legend: { '=': { floor: 16 }, '^': { stairs: 'n', floor: 16 }, g: { mark: 'gem' }, s: { mark: 'slime' }, '@': { mark: 'start' } } };\nlet world, hero, slimes, gems, hp, done;\nconst move = [0, 0];\nconst play = {\n  view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter', 'topdown'], pausable: true, touch: ['jump'], camera: { zoom: [.6, 2] },\n  enter() {\n    world.reset(); const rnd = E.rng(1), s = world.marks.start[0];\n    hero = world.hero({ x: s.x, y: s.y, rig: new E.Humanoid({ colors: { cloth: '#2f8f86' } }) });\n    slimes = world.marks.slime.map(m => world.mob({ x: m.x, y: m.y, blob: new E.Blob({ R: 6 }) }));\n    gems = world.marks.gem.map(m => world.thing('gem', m.x, m.y, m.z + 2, { spin: .5, bob: 3 }));\n    world.camera.follow(hero); hp = 3; done = false;\n    for (const e of slimes) e.data.t = rnd() * 2;\n  },\n  update(dt) {\n    if (game.input.pressed('camera')) world.camera.set(world.camera.mode === 'view' ? 'chase' : 'view');\n    world.camera.ground(game.input.move(), move);\n    hero.vx = E.approach(hero.vx, move[0] * 90, 900 * dt); hero.vy = E.approach(hero.vy, move[1] * 90, 900 * dt);\n    if (Math.hypot(move[0], move[1]) > .1) hero.facing = Math.atan2(move[1], move[0]);\n    if (hero.grounded && game.input.pressed('jump')) hero.jump(170);\n    for (const e of slimes) {   // hop at the hero now and then; touch one: a heart lost, knocked back\n      if ((e.data.t -= dt) < 0 && e.grounded) { e.data.t = 1.2; const a = Math.atan2(hero.y - e.y, hero.x - e.x); e.vx = Math.cos(a) * 60; e.vy = Math.sin(a) * 60; e.vz = 140; e.facing = a; }\n      if (Math.hypot(e.x - hero.x, e.y - hero.y) < 11 && Math.abs(e.z - hero.z) < 10 && !(hero.data.inv > 0)) { hp--; hero.data.inv = 1; hero.push((hero.x - e.x) * 12, (hero.y - e.y) * 12, 120); game.shake(3); }\n    }\n    hero.data.inv = (hero.data.inv || 0) - dt;\n    for (const t of world.touching(hero, 'gem')) { world.remove(t); gems.splice(gems.indexOf(t), 1); game.particles.glints(t.x, t.y, t.z + 6, 8, '#bff8ff'); }\n    if (!done && (!gems.length || hp <= 0)) { done = true; game.note(gems.length ? 'OUCH!' : 'ALL GEMS!', 2); game.after(2, () => game.go('play')); }\n    world.step(dt);\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    world.draw(r);\n    r.overlay(g => { E.ui.hearts(g, 6, 6, Math.max(0, hp), 3); E.font.text(g, 'GEMS ' + gems.length, 6, 18, '#bff8ff'); });\n  }\n};\ngame.start({ scenes: { play }, scene: 'play', boot: { steps: [\n  ['The 3D engine', () => E.World3D.init(game)],\n  ['The level', () => { world = new E.World3D(game, { level: LEVEL }); world.prepare(); }]] } });\n```\n\n## The level: text, one character per tile; row 0 is north\nlevel: { rows, legend, ground, side, colors, bottom }. A legend entry says what its character is (the defaults: '#' wall\n48, 'w' wall 12, 'P' pillar 56, '.' floor, ' ' pit, 't' brazier):\n  { floor: h }        walkable at height h (0 the ground; above 0 a raised floor, solid to the ground)\n  { wall: h }         a solid block h tall, cut low where it stands between a view camera and the level (cut: false)\n  { pillar: h }       each block of these tiles is one pillar, cut to a stump when it hides the target\n  { stairs: 'n'|'s'|'e'|'w', from: 0, floor: 16 }   rising toward that side, from height `from` to `floor`\n  { platform: h, thick: 4 }   a slab in the air, its top at h (walk under it); add pit: true for nothing below\n  { pit: true }       no floor: a body that falls in is lost (actor.fell)\n  { torch: true }     a brazier with a flickering light (the lights go to the torches nearest the target)\n  { mark: 'name' }    a spot: world.marks.name -> [{ x, y, z, cx, cy }] (the tile's centre, on its surface)\n  { shape: 'bench', facing, scale, mats, solid }   a shape standing on the tile (world.shape), walked round when solid\nand its look: ground ('flagstone' 'grass' 'dirt' 'sand' 'snow' 'planks' 'tiles' 'plain' 'water' 'lava', or (x, y) =>\n[r, g, b]) with colors { base, dark, light }, side ('brick' 'block' 'earth' 'planks' 'metal') with color (and top, line).\nThe level's ground is every tile's default; its side styles the floors' edges, its wallSide the walls ('brick'; a tile's\nown side wins). Add looks: E.World3D.GROUND.name, E.World3D.SIDE.name.\n\n## The world: new E.World3D(game, o)\no: { level, look: 'card' | 'puppet', res: 'pixels' | 'balanced' | 'full', gravity: 480, sky: '#06050b' or [top,\nhorizon], fog: { color, near, far } (units past the target) or false, ambient: [sky, ground, intensity], sun: { color,\nintensity, dir: [x, y, z] (it shines along it), area, shadow }, torchLights: 6, torchShadows: 1, torchPower: 30,\nlights: 2 (your own), spots: 0 (flashlights), glowLights: 4, shadows, outlines, filter, camera: { mode, proj, fov, chase, ... }, terrain (an open\nworld instead of the level: below), overlay: { fov } (first person: below), onDraw: (world, r) => {} (your own 3D drawing\neach frame, whichever scene draws the world) }\n  world.step(dt) / world.draw(r) / world.reset() / world.hash([your numbers]) / world.prepare() (build the shaders now)\n  world.dispose(): frees a world (its physics, meshes and textures); dispose a level's world before making the next\n  world.width, depth (units), mw, mh (tiles), bottom, marks, torches, actors, props, platforms, things, time, stats\n  world.ground(x, y)   the ground's height (a wall's top on a wall; bottom over a pit)\n  world.floorAt(x, y, z)   the surface a body at height z stands on or falls to (platforms too)\n  world.topH(x, y)     the top of whatever stands there (shots stop below it)\n  world.tile(cx, cy), spec(cx, cy), solidTile(cx, cy)   the level's character, its legend entry, walls and pillars\n  world.flow() -> flow: flow.to(x, y) aims it; flow.dir(x, y, out) -> the way there ([x, y], or null: go straight);\n      it climbs stairs and gentle slopes and drops off ledges, never jumps\n  world.randomFloor(rnd, minDist, fx, fy) -> [x, y]; world.clear(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1) -> a clear line of sight?\n  world.ray(x, y, z, dx, dy, dz, len, { skip: actor(s), level: true (sight lines), down }) -> the first hit { x, y, z, nx,\n      ny, nz, dist, actor, prop, ground } or null: hitscan, ledges, feet on the ground\n  world.block({ x, y, z, w, d, h, yaw } | { x, y, z, r, h }): an invisible fixed collider (walls you draw your own way;\n      z its base, as for world.box, world.thing and world.shape)\n  world.near(x, y, r, kind) -> actors within r; world.touching(actor, kind) -> things it touches\n  world.project(x, y, z) -> [x, y] in game pixels for the HUD (null behind the camera)\n  world.remove(actor | prop | platform | thing)\n\n## Actors: world.hero(o) and world.mob(o)\nhero: a kinematic capsule walked by Rapier's character controller: it slides along walls, climbs stairs and slopes,\nsteps up curbs, rides moving platforms, pushes crates and shoves mobs. mob: a dynamic body; the crowd pushes and piles\nup by itself, knockback is momentum. o: { x, y, z, rig: new E.Humanoid(...) | blob: new E.Blob(...) | orb: { color, r },\nr, half (the capsule: radius, half its straight part; half 0 a ball), facing, fly (no gravity), mass, trail (a color for\nthe rig's weapon trail), look ('card' | 'puppet' for this one), shadow, pose: false (you pose it yourself) }\nEach step you set what it wants: a.vx, a.vy (units a second on the ground: E.approach toward a target speed), a.jump(v)\nor a.vz, a.facing, a.anim (what its rig does: { attack: move.state, pose: 'cheer' | 'die' | 'cast', hurt, dash, expr };\na blob's { squash, look: [dx, dy], squint, flap }); world.step moves it, reads back a.x, a.y, a.z (its feet), a.vx,\na.vy, a.vz, a.grounded, a.landed (this step), a.fell (dropped below the level), and poses its rig.\n  a.place(x, y, z), a.push(vx, vy, vz) (knockback), a.setSolid(false) (fallen: it stops blocking the others), a.remove(),\n  a.resize(half) (a crouch, a slide: the capsule shorter, the feet kept; look up with world.ray before standing)\n  a.flash (a hit flash while above 0: count it down yourself, a.flash -= dt), a.fade (0..1), a.sink (units drawn into the floor: spawning, sinking away), a.visible,\n  a.data (yours). E.Attack, E.Combo and E.MOVES work as in 2D: attack.hits(list, e => E.inArc(a, a.facing, e, range,\n  half), e => ...) on actors.\n\n## Props, platforms and things\n  world.shape(id, x, y, z, { facing, scale, mats, flip, params, solid: true }): a shape (docs/ASSETS.md: an E.SHAPES id,\n  a store set's 'SET:id' after E.addAssets, or an entry) in 3D, from the 2D engine's parts and materials, solid as\n  map.solid's box. A reset takes yours away (the legend's stand again); world.shapes holds them; world.remove(s)\n  world.crate(x, y, z, yaw), world.barrel(x, y, z): dynamic bodies (z their centre; left out, on the floor); world.knock(p, fromX, fromY, dv, up) throws one;\n  world.box({ x, y, z, w, d, h, color, side, fixed, mass }); a prop has x, y, z, q (its rotation), data\n  world.platform({ w, d, h, path: [[x, y, z], ...] (its top's points), speed, wait, loop, side, color }): a platform that\n  moves (there and back, or round with loop); heroes on it ride it, bodies on it are carried\n  world.thing(kind, x, y, z, o): drawn by code: coin gem heart key star potion chest door flag sign lever pad spring\n  tree bush rock mushroom fence crystal trunk (an item box) typewriter (a save point); o: { yaw, spin (turns a second),\n  bob, size, color, open (0..1), on, solid: true | [w, d, h] (a block where it stands: world.setSolid(t, false) opens\n  it), data }. Add a kind:\n  E.World3D.THINGS.lamp = (pen, t, time) => { pen.tube(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 20, 1, '#555').glow(0, 0, 21, 2, '#ffd36a'); }\n  A door: { w (32, across its local x), h (40), swing: true (leaves on hinges; else a gate that rises), locked: 'iron' (a\n  key's id) }. world.reach(hero, 14, kinds) -> the thing in front of it within reach (what a check button acts on);\n  world.unlock(door, inv) -> 'open' | 'unlocked' (the key taken from an E.RPG inventory) | 'locked';\n  world.through(door, hero, { then }): the door opening between rooms (Resident Evil's): the camera goes through as it\n  swings open, the hero comes out beyond it; while world.passing the world holds still: skip your rules\n\n## Drawing for one frame (in draw, before world.draw(r))\n  world.arc(x, y, z, r0, r1, a0, a1, color, alpha): a flat arc on the ground (a telegraph)\n  world.glow(x, y, z, r, color, { core, light: true }): a glowing ball (a bolt); the first four asked for light the scene\n  world.pen.at(x, y, z, yaw, size, pitch, roll).box(x, y, z, w, d, h, color).ball(x, y, z, rx, ry, rz, color).tube(x0, y0, z0, x1,\n      y1, z1, r, color).cone(...base to tip).bar(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, w, t, color).glow(x, y, z, r, color)\n      .slab(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, ax, ay, az, w, t, color) (a box from one point to the other, w wide along a: any lean)\n  world.light({ color, reach, power, flicker }) (made once, with the world): a light; set its x, y, z and on each frame\n  world.flashlight(hero, { color, reach, angle, power, flicker: 0..1 (a failing bulb) }) (a world made with { spots: 1 }):\n  a spot from its hand along its facing, every frame, casting shadows; .on\n  game.particles (sparks, dust, bits, rings, glints, fire, text): drawn in 3D; they land on the level; text goes on the HUD\n  game.shake(n), game.flash(color), game.freeze(s), game.note(text): as in 2D\n\n## The camera: world.camera\n  modes: 'view' (the 2D engine's views as real 3D cameras following camera.target: game.view (game.nextView()),\n  game.yaw (game.rotateView(45)): the starter's shell binds V and [ ] to them, the engine no key; game.zoom (the wheel,\n  a pinch, Settings, where the scene's camera.zoom allows); proj 'persp' | 'ortho'; dist; walls in\n  front cut low), 'chase' (behind the target; it swings round behind as the target moves; a click captures the mouse to\n  turn it), 'first' (the target's eyes), 'fly', 'fixed' (camera.fixed = { x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov }: your own,\n  roll as pen.at's), 'zones' (Resident Evil's: camera.zones = [{ box: [x0, y0, x1, y1], at: [x, y, z], look: [x, y, z]\n  (fixed) | follow: true (turning after the target) | rail: [[x, y, z], [x, y, z]] (sliding level with it), fov (framed\n  for 4:3: a taller screen widens it) }]: the target's zone is the camera, cut once it is camera.margin (8) units in\n  (camera.zone, camera.cuts); across a cut ground() keeps the old way while the stick is held)\n  camera: { capture: true (a click captures the mouse in any mode; camera.takeMouse(out) -> its [dx, dy] since the last\n  call: turn your own camera), near, far (units) }; camera.target also sizes the outlines and centres the sun's shadow\n  camera.set(mode), camera.follow(actor), camera.ground(move, out) -> the stick turned to the ground,\n  camera.aim(actor) -> the angle the mouse (on the floor) or right stick points from it, or null; camera.heading,\n  camera.pose, camera.chase = { pitch, dist, fov, height, auto }, camera.first = { pitch, fov }\n  camera.steer(hero, move, dt, out) -> the way to go by the player's setting (game.settings.define(E.World3D.CONTROLS)):\n  ground(), or tank controls (up forward along hero.facing, down back, left and right turn it: set facing yourself only\n  when !camera.tank)\n  Lock-on (Zelda's targeting): camera.lock(hero, mobs, { range: 160 }) -> the one in sight (alive, in range, a clear\n  line) nearest where hero faces, or null; camera.cycle(mobs, 1 | -1) the next round the hero; camera.unlock();\n  camera.lockOn. The chase and first cameras swing behind the hero to frame both, the view frames both, ground() strafes\n  round it, a reticle marks it (camera.reticle: a color, false); it lets go when the target dies (alive = false), leaves\n  or goes past 1.25 times the range. Turn the hero to it yourself\n\n## Open worlds: new E.World3D(game, { terrain: { height: (x, y) => z, ... } })\nterrain: { size (32768: two kilometres), height, color: (x, y, z, nz, out) => [r, g, b] (0..255), cell (32), chunk (32\ncells), far (draw distance), physics (heightfields this near an actor), detail (1.7), rock: { colors, band, slope: [s0,\ns1] } (strata where it steepens), speckle, mottle, bottom }. Streamed: a chunk is sampled when first asked about, its\ncollider made near actors (world.step), its meshes near the camera (world.draw: finer near). world.ground, floorAt, topH\nand world.terrain.normal(x, y) read the triangles Rapier collides with; flow() goes straight. sky: { top, horizon, ground,\nsun, haze } is a dome; fog: { color, near, far }. Keep height() fast: it runs per sample.\n\n## Vehicles, tracks, flight (the RACING and FLIGHT slices show them)\nworld.vehicle({ kind: 'kart' | 'car' | 'hover', x, y, z, angle, rig (its driver, seated), color, and the drive module's\nE.Car options over the kind's (top, accel, brake, turn, grip, drift, hop...), surfaces, ground: (x, y) => tag }): a ball\nRapier rolls (walls, bumps, ramps, jumps) steered by E.Car's arcade handling; an actor. Each step v.update(dt, null,\n{ steer, gas, brake, drift }) (or an input), then world.step; it reads speed, slide, angle, drifting, charge (a kart's\ndrift boost), boostT, onGround, surface ('road', 'boost', a track's off, the level's ground: grass, sand... slow it),\nbumped; v.boost(sec, power), v.spinOut(sec). E.Race (the drive module) races vehicles as it races E.Cars.\nworld.track({ path: new E.Path(points [x, y, z], { closed: true, smooth: 'catmull' }), width: 96, bank: .25 (radians at\nthe tightest bends), walls: true, kerb: 8, pads: [[d, across]] (boost pads), gaps: [[d0, d1, side]] (no wall: a\nshortcut's way off), off (the surface beside it, 'grass'; null: the ground's own), surface: 'road' (a shortcut's\n'dirt'), color, line }): the road and its walls, drawn\nand solid (heights in the path: hills, a hump to jump; banked about the low edge); track.at(d) -> { x, y, z, angle, bank\n}, track.where(x, y) -> { d, across, z }, track.surface(x, y). A terrain under it keeps below it (your height function).\nworld.flyer({ x, y, z, yaw, speed: 300, min, max (brake, boost), turn, climb, bank, invert, low (least height), bounds:\n/* =====================================================================================\n * SLICE 7  CRAWLER  (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder, King's Field). @modules grid\n * The Lantern Vaults in first person, a cell at a time: W and S (or up and down) step forward and back, A and D turn a\n * quarter turn, Q and E step aside, each one tweened. A party of four, their portraits under the view: two in front who\n * fight, two behind who cast. J (or a click on the view) uses what is in front (a lever, a locked gate) or has the next\n * one ready act: a blow at what stands in front, else the mage's bolt; K the mage's bolt down the corridor, H the\n * cleric's heal, 1 to 4 (or a click on a portrait) that one's own act; Tab the map (what the party has seen: E.Fog).\n * Gates that rise (one with an iron lock), a lever on a wall, plates in the floor (one raises a gate, one springs darts);\n * wisps, slimes, the dead of the vaults and the Warden at the bottom; the stair out behind it. Settings, Turns: REAL TIME\n * (each one's gauge fills on game time, E.Turns 'atb': Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) or TURNS (the vaults wait:\n * each step, turn or blow is the party's turn, the monsters act by their speed, E.Turns 'speed': Wizardry).\n * THE 3D: the camera 'fixed' at the party's eyes, tweened per step; a level of 2 x 2 tiles a cell, braziers in niches,\n * a ceiling and the party's lantern; world.thing gates that rise, and a lever, plates and a stair drawn by the slice;\n * mobs walked cell to cell. Deep link: #crawler/warden (the Warden's hall, its gates open). CRAWLER.run(n): a scripted\n * walk's hash (the proof); CRAWLER.bot(): to the Warden and out\n * ===================================================================================== */\nconst CRAWLER = (() => {\n  const C = 32, PI = Math.PI, EYE = 25, STEP_T = .28, TURN_T = .2, MOVE_T = .4;\n  // the four ways, a quarter turn apart: 0 north, 1 east, 2 south, 3 west\n  const DX = [0, 1, 0, -1], DY = [-1, 0, 1, 0], YAW = d => -PI / 2 + d * PI / 2, WAYS = ['N', 'E', 'S', 'W'];\n  /* ---- THE VAULTS: one character a cell (2 metres: 2 x 2 of the level's tiles). # wall, . floor, @ the start (facing\n   * north), n a wall with a brazier in a niche, L a wall with a lever, G a gate (raised by what is wired to it), K a gate\n   * with an iron lock, P a plate (raises a gate), T a plate that springs darts, X the stair out, i the iron key, h a\n   * flask, w wisps, s slimes, k the dead, W the Warden ---- */\n  const MAP = [\n    '#########n#n###n###n#',\n    '#######.....i.#..X..#',\n    '#######....k..#..W..#',\n    '#######.s.....#.....#',\n    '#.....#.....s.###G###',\n    'n.w.h.#.......#..P..n',\n    '#w....#.......#...k.#',\n    '###.######.######K###',\n    '###............T..###',\n    '######n###G##########',\n    '########.....########',\n    '#######L.....n#######',\n    '########..@..########',\n    '#####################'];\n  const MW = MAP[0].length, MH = MAP.length, at = (x, y) => (MAP[y] || '')[x] || '#';\n  const WALLS = '#nL', wallCell = (x, y) => WALLS.includes(at(x, y));\n  // what is wired to what: a lever's or a plate's cell -> the gate it raises\n  const WIRES = { '7,11': '10,9', '17,5': '17,4' };\n  // a wall cell's open side (a niche's brazier, a lever's face): the first way with floor beyond it\n  const openSide = (x, y) => [0, 1, 2, 3].find(d => !wallCell(x + DX[d], y + DY[d]));\n  // the level: each cell 2 x 2 tiles; a niche holds a brazier ('t') in the tile on its open side\n  const NICHE = [['t#', '##'], ['#t', '##'], ['##', 't#'], ['t#', '##']];\n  const rows = [];\n  for (let y = 0; y < MH; y++) for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    let row = ''; for (let x = 0; x < MW; x++) { const c = at(x, y); row += c === 'n' ? NICHE[openSide(x, y)][k] : wallCell(x, y) ? '##' : '..'; }\n    rows.push(row);\n  }\n  const LEVEL = { rows, ground: 'flagstone', colors: { base: '#4a4656', dark: '#34303e', light: '#5a5668' }, side: 'brick', color: '#4e4858', legend: {} };\n  const mid = c => c * C + C / 2;\n\n  /* ---- THE PARTY: two in front who fight, two behind who cast; each one's portrait is its rig's (drawPortrait) ---- */\n  const PARTY = [\n    { name: 'BRAM', role: 'FIGHTER', hp: 46, speed: 100, hit: [8, 13], rig: { build: 'heroic', hat: { style: 'helmet', color: '#8a92a0' }, colors: { cloth: '#7a3a2e', pants: '#3a3440', hair: '#4a2e1e', skin: '#d8a888', metal: '#b8c0cc' } } },\n    { name: 'TESK', role: 'ROGUE', hp: 34, speed: 135, hit: [5, 10], rig: { hood: true, colors: { cloth: '#3a5a3e', pants: '#2e2a26', hair: '#2a3a2c', skin: '#c89070' } } },\n    { name: 'MAREN', role: 'CLERIC', hp: 30, mp: 16, speed: 90, heal: [12, 19], rig: { hair: 'long', hat: 'band', colors: { cloth: '#d8d0b8', pants: '#8a7a5a', hair: '#c8a050', skin: '#e8c0a0', hat: '#c8a040' } } },\n    { name: 'QUILL', role: 'MAGE', hp: 22, mp: 18, speed: 85, bolt: [11, 18], rig: { hat: 'pointed', weapon: null, colors: { cloth: '#3a3a7a', pants: '#2a2a4a', hair: '#e0e0e8', skin: '#e0b090', hat: '#3a3a7a' } } }];\n  const BOLT_MP = 4, HEAL_MP = 3;\n  /* ---- THE MONSTERS: hp, a blow's damage, speed (100: a turn as the party's), how far they see (cells) ---- */\n  const BONE = { bone: '#d8cfb4', cloth: '#2c2434', belt: '#5a4a38', metal: '#b3b8c0', metalDk: '#636872', hilt: '#6a5038' };\n  const WARDEN = { cloth: '#3a3a52', pants: '#2a2832', boot: '#1e1c24', belt: '#5a4a30', trim: '#9a8a5a', metal: '#7a8294', metalDk: '#3e4452', skin: '#8a9aa8', hair: '#1a1a22' };\n  const KINDS = {\n    wisp: { hp: 9, dmg: [2, 4], speed: 150, sight: 4, z: 16 },\n    slime: { hp: 20, dmg: [3, 6], speed: 70, sight: 3 },\n    skeleton: { hp: 28, dmg: [4, 8], speed: 100, sight: 5, move: 'slash' },\n    warden: { hp: 120, dmg: [6, 10], speed: 95, sight: 6, move: 'overhead', boss: true }\n  };\n  const MARK = { w: 'wisp', s: 'slime', k: 'skeleton', W: 'warden' };\n\n  /* ---- THINGS drawn by this slice (the engine's kinds are in its manual): a lever on a wall, a plate in the floor, the\n   * stair out (an arch over steps down, lit once the Warden falls) ---- */\n  W3.THINGS.wallLever = (p, t) => {   // its local +y points out of the wall\n    const a = t.on ? -1 : 1;\n    p.box(0, .8, 20, 6, 1.6, 9, '#4a4452').box(0, 1.8, 20, 2, .8, 5.5, '#24202a')\n      .tube(0, 2.2, 20, 0, 3.6, 20 + a * 5, .55, '#a8a8b8').ball(0, 3.8, 20 + a * 5.4, 1.1, 1.1, 1.1, t.on ? '#4ae070' : '#e8483a');\n  };\n  W3.THINGS.plate = (p, t) => { const s = t.on ? .2 : 1, c = t.color || '#6a6474'; p.box(0, 0, s / 2, 22, 22, s, c).box(0, 0, s + .05, 17, 17, .1, E.shade(c, -.2)); };\n  W3.THINGS.stair = (p, t) => {\n    const s = '#5a5468';\n    p.box(-13, 0, 19, 4, 5, 38, s).box(13, 0, 19, 4, 5, 38, s).box(0, 0, 39, 30, 6, 4, s);\n    for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) p.box(0, -2 - k * 3, -1 - k * 2.5, 22, 3, 2, '#3a3644');   // the steps going down\n    if (t.on) p.glow(0, 0, 16, 5 + Math.sin((t.t || 0) * 3) * .8, '#9ae8ff').halo(0, 0, 16, 18, [.15, .3, .38]);\n    else p.box(0, 0, 18, 22, 1, 34, '#14121a');\n  };\n\n  /* ---- SOUNDS AND MUSIC (the step sequencer's format: original songs) ---- */\n  const SOUNDS = {\n    step: { wave: 'noise', freq: 320, to: 120, dur: .07, vol: .1, filter: 'lowpass' }, turn: { wave: 'noise', freq: 900, to: 500, dur: .06, vol: .05 },\n    thud: [{ wave: 'square', freq: 90, to: 50, dur: .1, vol: .2 }, { wave: 'noise', freq: 300, dur: .06, vol: .12 }],\n    swing: { wave: 'noise', freq: 2600, to: 800, dur: .1, vol: .12 }, bolt: [{ wave: 'saw', freq: 220, to: 880, dur: .18, vol: .16 }, { wave: 'noise', freq: 3000, to: 600, dur: .2, vol: .1 }],\n    lever: [{ wave: 'square', freq: 260, to: 140, dur: .08, vol: .2 }, { wave: 'noise', freq: 1200, dur: .05, vol: .12, delay: .09 }],\n    gate: { wave: 'saw', freq: 70, to: 110, dur: .9, vol: .12, vib: [12, .1], filter: 'lowpass' }, plate: { wave: 'square', freq: 140, to: 90, dur: .1, vol: .2 },\n    dart: [{ wave: 'noise', freq: 4000, to: 2000, dur: .05, vol: .2 }, { wave: 'noise', freq: 4000, to: 2000, dur: .05, vol: .2, delay: .07 }],\n    locked: { wave: 'square', freq: 180, dur: .05, vol: .16 }, unlock: { wave: 'square', freq: 660, arp: [0, 7], step: .06, dur: .14, vol: .18 },\n    rattle: { wave: 'noise', freq: 2200, arp: [0, 3, 0, 5], step: .04, dur: .2, vol: .1 }, squelch: { wave: 'sine', freq: 140, to: 60, dur: .16, vol: .25 },\n    zap: { wave: 'square', freq: 1400, to: 300, dur: .1, vol: .1 }, roar: [{ wave: 'saw', freq: 110, to: 55, dur: 1, vol: .2, vib: [7, .12] }, { wave: 'noise', freq: 500, to: 120, dur: .8, vol: .15, filter: 'lowpass' }]\n  };\n  const VAULT = { bpm: 72, steps: 4, tracks: [   // the vaults: a slow figure over a drone, water dripping\n    { wave: 'triangle', vol: .26, notes: 'A1 - - - - - - - | A1 - - - - - - - | F1 - - - - - - - | E1 - - - - - - -' },\n    { wave: 'pulse25', vol: .05, notes: 'A3 . C4 . E4 . . . | D4 . C4 . B3 . . . | A3 . C4 . F4 . E4 . | E4 . . . G#3 . . .' },\n    { wave: 'sine', vol: .07, notes: '. . . . . . E5 . | . . . . . . . . | . . . . . . . A5 | . . . . . . . .' }] };\n  const FIGHT = { bpm: 128, steps: 4, tracks: [   // the Warden\n    { wave: 'saw', vol: .06, notes: 'A3 A3 . A3 C4 . A3 . | G3 G3 . G3 Bb3 . G3 . | F3 F3 . F3 A3 . F3 . | E3 . G#3 . B3 . E4 .' },\n    { wave: 'triangle', vol: .3, notes: 'A1 . A1 . A1 . A1 . | G1 . G1 . G1 . G1 . | F1 . F1 . F1 . F1 . | E1 . E1 . E1 . E1 .' },\n    { wave: 'drums', vol: .1, notes: 'k . h k s . h . | k k h . s . h h' }] };\n\n  /* ---- THE STATE ---- */\n  let world = null, hero = null, lamp = null, grid = null, fog = null, turns = null, rnd = Math.random, mode = 'real', scripted = false;\n  let party = null, S = null, foes = [], gates = {}, levers = [], plates = {}, items = [], stair = null, bolts = [], keys = null;\n  let total = 0, msg = null, showMap = false, song = null, ended = 0, slashT = 0, phase = 'party', took = 0, rounds = 0;\n  BOOT.push(['The vaults', () => {\n    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(SOUNDS)) A.define(k, v);\n    world = new W3(game, { level: LEVEL, torchLights: 4, torchShadows: 0, torchPower: 16, torchReach: 160, torchColor: '#ffb070', lights: 2,\n      ambient: ['#5a5478', '#221e2a', 3.4], fog: { color: '#050407', near: 40, far: 250 }, sky: '#050407', camera: { mode: 'fixed' },\n      onDraw: w => {   // the ceiling over the vaults, the bolts in flight, the wisps' zaps\n        w.pen.at().box(w.width / 2, w.depth / 2, 50, w.width, w.depth, 4, '#1c1a22');   // (at(): the pen's origin back at the world's)\n        for (const b of bolts) w.glow(b.x, b.y, b.z, 3.2, '#ffb14a', { light: true });\n        for (const e of foes) if (e.zap > 0) w.pen.at().bar(e.x, e.y, e.z + 2, S.x, S.y, EYE - 6, .6, .6, '#bff4ff');\n      } });\n  }]);\n\n  /** a fresh descent (seed: the dice; m: 'real' or 'turns', else the player's setting) */\n  function reset(seed = 1, m = null) {\n    world.reset(); rnd = E.rng(seed); mode = m || game.settings.crawlTurns || 'real';\n    const st = MAP.findIndex(r => r.includes('@')), sx = MAP[st].indexOf('@');\n    hero = world.hero({ x: mid(sx), y: mid(st), r: 4, visible: false }); world.camera.follow(hero);\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat vendor/engine/README.md && sed -n '1,245p' vendor/hub/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat vendor/engine/README.md && sed -n '1,245p' vendor/hub/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.89.1)\n\nEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\nfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n\n**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\nis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\nare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\nlacks.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n   `game/` and make it yours.\n3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n   before you use it.\n7. **A game in real 3D** (first person, a light gun, a racer on banked roads, flight, a dungeon crawled in 3D, fixed\n   cameras): the 2D views have no perspective camera, but the engine has a 3D add-on, `World3D` (three.js r182 and\n   Rapier 0.19.3 under the 2D engine, which keeps the HUD, menus and sound). It is not in this folder: take\n   `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (its header is its manual), `vendor/` and the 3D kit closest to your game\n   (`src/starter3d/`) from the repository, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge, and see them play at\n   my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d.\n\n## What is here\n\n| File | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n\nNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\nengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n\n## Run, test, check\n\n```sh\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\nnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\nnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\nnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n```\n\nOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\nthe two scripts into the page, writing every `</script` inside them as `<\\/script` (the agent edition's header has one,\nand the page would end there).\n\n## Testing a game without a browser\n\n`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped unless\nthe game is made with `draw: true` (the template is): then each `advance` draws a frame on a stand-in, and a draw error\nlands in `game.errors`, as it would on a player's screen.\n\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n- `game.go('play')` fades for `game.fadeTime` (.22 s) before the new scene enters: advance past it, or set\n  `game.fadeTime = 0` in a test.\n- A scene change clears `game.after` and `game.every` timers (`{ keep: true }` outlives it); those made before the first\n  scene live into it. It ends a hit-stop; `game.timeScale` stays (restore a slow motion with a kept timer). A boot step's `game.go(name)` enters that scene when the boot ends.\n- Draw changes nothing, and nothing your update or input relies on (a click target, what is on screen) may be made only\n  in `draw`: `E.Menu`'s rows come from its update (`menu.rowAt(i)` is entry i's box, for a test's click), and the camera\n  (`game.cam`) says where the screen is in update.\n- `E.store` keeps values for the visit in Node, which has no `localStorage` (`E.store.clear()` between games in one test).\n- A script's `tap` and `hold` are no device: `input.label()` keeps showing the keys or buttons the player used last.\n\nThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card, longer if one asks for more time: what\n   the game is, how to win, the controls. The card is the hub's, from the\n   game's tags, or the game's own (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. A player can ask for more time, which adds\nten seconds; while more than half of them are asking, the countdown holds.\nBy default the hub shows its own card over the game. A game can make the\ncard its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
