{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"ls evidence/*delivered* evidence/*final* 2>/dev/null || true\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('tools/browser-test.py');s=p.read_text()\ns=s.replace(\\\"page.keyboard.press('w');page.wait_for_timeout(180)\\\",\\\"page.keyboard.press('w');page.wait_for_function('__app.player.y===8',timeout=5000)\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"page.keyboard.press('e');page.wait_for_timeout(100)\\\",\\\"page.keyboard.press('e');page.wait_for_function('__app.player.dir===1',timeout=5000)\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"page.keyboard.press('q');page.wait_for_timeout(100)\\\",\\\"page.keyboard.press('q');page.wait_for_function('__app.player.dir===0',timeout=5000)\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"page.keyboard.press('Tab');page.wait_for_timeout(160)\\\",\\\"page.keyboard.press('Tab');page.wait_for_function('__app.ui.shell.screen===\\\\\\\"pack\\\\\\\"',timeout=5000)\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"page.keyboard.press('Escape');page.wait_for_timeout(100)\\\\n    page.keyboard.press('Escape');page.wait_for_timeout(150)\\\",\\\"page.keyboard.press('Escape');page.wait_for_function('\"'!__app.ui.shell.screen'\"',timeout=5000)\\\\n    page.keyboard.press('Escape');page.wait_for_function('__app.ui.shell.screen===\\\\\\\"pause\\\\\\\"',timeout=5000)\\\")\ns=s.replace(\\\"page.evaluate('__app.attractStep(24000)')\\\", \\\"page.evaluate('for(let i=0;i<4;i++)__app.attractStep(6001)')\\\")\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ncat >> docs/ISSUES.md <<'EOF'\n13. Brief keyboard movement taps could fall between frames because movement checked only held state: major input defect; movement now checks both fresh presses and held repeat. Browser assertions now wait for production state instead of assuming a software-rendered frame arrives within 150 ms.\nEOF\nnpm run build && npm run verify -- file touch\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat >> src/ui/style.css <<'EOF'\n@media(min-width:1500px){:root{--body:32px}.esh.danger-shell button{font-size:.9em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-m{font-size:.88em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-ttl .esh-btns button{font-size:.9em}.intent{font-size:.82em}.enemy-pools{font-size:.8em}.readiness{font-size:.65em}.meter-head{font-size:.75em}.hand{font-size:.75em}#recent{font-size:.64em}.goal{font-size:.66em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-ttl .esh-sub{font-size:.95em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-ttl .esh-btns{margin-top:2vh}.esh.danger-shell .esh-tags{font-size:.45em}}\nEOF\ncat > docs/RESEARCH.md <<'EOF'\n# Research and provenance\n\nInspected 2026-11-06. Source: **Advanced Dungeons & Dragons: Treasure of Tarmin (1983)**.\n\n- [Scanned Mattel manual](https://mocagh.org/intvaction/addtot-manual.pdf): downloaded and extracted all 17 pages to manual-text.txt; read controls, opening equipment, dual score channels, pack/hand layout, rest, doors/ladders/gates, weapons, armor/rings, books, monsters, and actual treasure-claim ending. Visually inspected the two-page screen/control spread (PDF page3); maps and original geometric first-person drawing appear in it. The scan is research material, not a shipped game asset.\n- [Official manual transcript](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/instructions/addtarmininst.html): cross-check of controls, six pack positions, clicks for counting supplies, one-way traversal, War/Spiritual distinctions, enemy classes and objective. Original short instruction style informed terse new prose. This game uses original authored maps, names, balance and cooperative rules rather than claiming numerical fidelity.\n- [Publisher history](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/addtarmin.html): confirms Mattel cartridge5300, release1983, APh production, programmerTomLoughry and original package illustratorJerrolRichardson.\n- [Actual gameplay recording](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZqhCM5Ntllo): retrieved 00:40–01:20 with yt-dlp, inspected decoded stills at00:43,00:58,01:12. Observed flat rectilinear first-person corridor framing, colored rooms, distinct silhouette in front, separate held-object icons and dual numbers. This was a 40-second excerpt, not a claim to reviewing the entire 32-minute recording. Exact original timbres and music remain **unknown**; no human listening was available. The manual documents counting clicks and suction/other magical traversal sounds. All shipped music, effects and gibberish are original synthesized code.\n\n## Shipped foundation\n\n| Component | Pin | Source / license |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| my-3D2dge handoff / World3D | v0.89.1;8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691 | [engine repository](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691),MIT |\n| three.js |0.182.0|[three.js](https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/tree/r182),MIT|\n| Rapier SIMD compat |0.19.3|[Dimforge](https://github.com/dimforge/rapier.js),Apache-2.0; embedded WASM|\n| AIHub Session Protocol |v1,client1.4.0;35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869|[Hub contracts](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub); session.js copied unmodified|\n\nRead pinned handoff README/API/ASSETS, World3D header/lifecycle/implementation, crawler shell, native rigs/moves/overlay and native E.Shell/settings/rebinding contracts. Read Hub README/session/bridge/round/arena/harness. Grid cell32,eye25,axes x-east/y-south/z-up,16units/metre, interpolated navigation follow the crawler foundation. Rendering and native animation are actual engine calls; deterministic game rules own collisions, damage and turns. Native core/add-on files are unmodified; game extensions register shapes, things, rig poses and instruments.\n\nThe title was selected with Python secrets.choice from exactly50 original riffs. Selected title: **Advanced Dungeons and Danger**. No runtime external art, fonts, models, audio, CDN modules, JSON or WASM files are needed; all dependencies are embedded in the delivered HTML. Research files and developer source are not runtime dependencies.\nEOF\ncat > docs/CONTROLS.md <<'EOF'\n# Controls\n\nWASD/arrow keys step relative to heading. Q/E quarter-turn. Mouse: double-click the view to capture, move to look; Escape frees the cursor. With a free cursor, click objects/context actions and menus; middle-drag looks. F interacts; Enter/left click commits the chosen attack in combat. Right click/G guards. 1 chooses basic War,2 basic Spirit,3 held weapon. Tab/I pack,X swap,M map,H rest,U use held utility,V inspect,T next target,R retreat,Escape/P menu.\n\nStandard mapping controller: left stick step, right stick look,L1/R1 quarter-turn,R2 commit,L2 guard,Cross interact/confirm,Triangle pack,Square swap,Circle back/retreat,Touchpad map,Options menu. Create+Options remains reserved to AIHub. PS glyphs default; Settings can show Xbox and rebind supported actions. Disconnect does not discard your expedition.\n\nTouch: left-half drag steps; right-half drag looks; contextual prompt interacts/commits. Double-tap right side opens pack; two-finger tap opens map. Menu remains in the corner. Touch assistance selects useful channel choices; no permanent stick/button bank. The same pack/map/settings support tapping.\n\nCombat actions are deliberate; selecting an attack does not commit it. Solo has no turn timer. In co-op the first commitment opens one host-owned8-second deadline; all living players ready resolves early. Uncommitted players guard on expiry. Settings are local and the online world remains live. Gate crossing is personal, one way; each adventurer selects their own channel balance. Descent requires both living players at the ladder and ready. A ration revives a nearby downed ally through the pack/context button; downed players do not block descent.\nEOF\ncat > README.md <<'EOF'\n# Advanced Dungeons and Danger\n\nOpen the delivered HTML in a current Chromium or Safari/WebKit browser. Everything needed to render and play is embedded; no neighboring source files or CDN access are required. The native renderer prefers WebGPU and falls back to WebGL2. A browser lacking both gets an actionable message.\n\nThe expedition has three authored floors, two survival strengths, a real six-slot pack, five ordinary enemy roles, a three-seal Minotaur and an explicit treasure claim. Solo progression and settings persist when storage permits. Export/import saves are in the pause menu. Co-op uses manual WebRTC invitations or AIHub's shared-world session lifecycle. No TURN relay is supplied, so restrictive NAT/firewalls may prevent a direct peer connection. No host migration is claimed.\n\nBuild: \"'`npm run build`. Verification: `npm run verify` prints failures and one count. Pure rules/protocol/package only: `npm run verify -- --rules`. Browser groups can be selected (`file touch rtc hub fallbacks`). Python Playwright uses the workstation'\"'s installed environment and browsers. Rebuild validates and imports a designer campaign with \"'`npm run build -- --content path/to/export.json`.\n\nDeveloper mode: Pause → Expedition tools. Live sliders, reset, floor/encounter jumps, invulnerability, supplies, item spawns, checkpoint retry, geometry reveal, state/intent diagnostics, deterministic step and content workshop all use the production model. Edits mark runs modified. In scored Hub play developer tools are read-only and title/restart/signaling are unavailable. The workshop previews tile/entity data, validates, imports/exports and applies edits to a new modified run.\n\nSee docs/CONTROLS.md, docs/RESEARCH.md, docs/rules.md, docs/network.md and docs/presentation.md. Canonical source/feedback/catalogue data is src/report.json; build embeds it and writes root exit-interview.json. PROGRESS.md in the workspace root records milestones. The declared10-minute Hub duration is a pacing target; automated route timings are machine-driven commands, not a human playtime measurement.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat >> src/ui/style.css <<'EOF'\n@media(min-width:1500px){:root{--body:32px}.esh.danger-shell button{font-size:.9em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-m{font-size:.88em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-ttl .esh-btns button{font-size:.9em}.intent{font-size:.82em}.enemy-pools{font-size:.8em}.readiness{font-size:.65em}.meter-head{font-size:.75em}.hand{font-size:.75em}#recent{font-size:.64em}.goal{font-size:.66em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-ttl .esh-sub{font-size:.95em}.esh.danger-shell .esh-ttl .esh-btns{margin-top:2vh}.esh.danger-shell .esh-tags{font-size:.45em}}\nEOF\ncat > docs/RESEARCH.md <<'EOF'\n# Research and provenance\n\nInspected 2026-11-06. Source: **Advanced Dungeons & Dragons: Treasure of Tarmin (1983)**.\n\n- [Scanned Mattel manual](https://mocagh.org/intvaction/addtot-manual.pdf): downloaded and extracted all 17 pages to manual-text.txt; read controls, opening equipment, dual score channels, pack/hand layout, rest, doors/ladders/gates, weapons, armor/rings, books, monsters, and actual treasure-claim ending. Visually inspected the two-page screen/control spread (PDF page3); maps and original geometric first-person drawing appear in it. The scan is research material, not a shipped game asset.\n- [Official manual transcript](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/instructions/addtarmininst.html): cross-check of controls, six pack positions, clicks for counting supplies, one-way traversal, War/Spiritual distinctions, enemy classes and objective. Original short instruction style informed terse new prose. This game uses original authored maps, names, balance and cooperative rules rather than claiming numerical fidelity.\n- [Publisher history](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/addtarmin.html): confirms Mattel cartridge5300, release1983, APh production, programmerTomLoughry and original package illustratorJerrolRichardson.\n- [Actual gameplay recording](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZqhCM5Ntllo): retrieved 00:40–01:20 with yt-dlp, inspected decoded stills at00:43,00:58,01:12. Observed flat rectilinear first-person corridor framing, colored rooms, distinct silhouette in front, separate held-object icons and dual numbers. This was a 40-second excerpt, not a claim to reviewing the entire 32-minute recording. Exact original timbres and music remain **unknown**; no human listening was available. The manual documents counting clicks and suction/other magical traversal sounds. All shipped music, effects and gibberish are original synthesized code.\n\n## Shipped foundation\n\n| Component | Pin | Source / license |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| my-3D2dge handoff / World3D | v0.89.1;8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691 | [engine repository](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691),MIT |\n| three.js |0.182.0|[three.js](https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/tree/r182),MIT|\n| Rapier SIMD compat |0.19.3|[Dimforge](https://github.com/dimforge/rapier.js),Apache-2.0; embedded WASM|\n| AIHub Session Protocol |v1,client1.4.0;35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869|[Hub contracts](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub); session.js copied unmodified|\n\nRead pinned handoff README/API/ASSETS, World3D header/lifecycle/implementation, crawler shell, native rigs/moves/overlay and native E.Shell/settings/rebinding contracts. Read Hub README/session/bridge/round/arena/harness. Grid cell32,eye25,axes x-east/y-south/z-up,16units/metre, interpolated navigation follow the crawler foundation. Rendering and native animation are actual engine calls; deterministic game rules own collisions, damage and turns. Native core/add-on files are unmodified; game extensions register shapes, things, rig poses and instruments.\n\nThe title was selected with Python secrets.choice from exactly50 original riffs. Selected title: **Advanced Dungeons and Danger**. No runtime external art, fonts, models, audio, CDN modules, JSON or WASM files are needed; all dependencies are embedded in the delivered HTML. Research files and developer source are not runtime dependencies.\nEOF\ncat > docs/CONTROLS.md <<'EOF'\n# Controls\n\nWASD/arrow keys step relative to heading. Q/E quarter-turn. Mouse: double-click the view to capture, move to look; Escape frees the cursor. With a free cursor, click objects/context actions and menus; middle-drag looks. F interacts; Enter/left click commits the chosen attack in combat. Right click/G guards. 1 chooses basic War,2 basic Spirit,3 held weapon. Tab/I pack,X swap,M map,H rest,U use held utility,V inspect,T next target,R retreat,Escape/P menu.\n\nStandard mapping controller: left stick step, right stick look,L1/R1 quarter-turn,R2 commit,L2 guard,Cross interact/confirm,Triangle pack,Square swap,Circle back/retreat,Touchpad map,Options menu. Create+Options remains reserved to AIHub. PS glyphs default; Settings can show Xbox and rebind supported actions. Disconnect does not discard your expedition.\n\nTouch: left-half drag steps; right-half drag looks; contextual prompt interacts/commits. Double-tap right side opens pack; two-finger tap opens map. Menu remains in the corner. Touch assistance selects useful channel choices; no permanent stick/button bank. The same pack/map/settings support tapping.\n\nCombat actions are deliberate; selecting an attack does not commit it. Solo has no turn timer. In co-op the first commitment opens one host-owned8-second deadline; all living players ready resolves early. Uncommitted players guard on expiry. Settings are local and the online world remains live. Gate crossing is personal, one way; each adventurer selects their own channel balance. Descent requires both living players at the ladder and ready. A ration revives a nearby downed ally through the pack/context button; downed players do not block descent.\nEOF\ncat > README.md <<'EOF'\n# Advanced Dungeons and Danger\n\nOpen the delivered HTML in a current Chromium or Safari/WebKit browser. Everything needed to render and play is embedded; no neighboring source files or CDN access are required. The native renderer prefers WebGPU and falls back to WebGL2. A browser lacking both gets an actionable message.\n\nThe expedition has three authored floors, two survival strengths, a real six-slot pack, five ordinary enemy roles, a three-seal Minotaur and an explicit treasure claim. Solo progression and settings persist when storage permits. Export/import saves are in the pause menu. Co-op uses manual WebRTC invitations or AIHub's shared-world session lifecycle. No TURN relay is supplied, so restrictive NAT/firewalls may prevent a direct peer connection. No host migration is claimed.\n\nBuild: \"'`npm run build`. Verification: `npm run verify` prints failures and one count. Pure rules/protocol/package only: `npm run verify -- --rules`. Browser groups can be selected (`file touch rtc hub fallbacks`). Python Playwright uses the workstation'\"'s installed environment and browsers. Rebuild validates and imports a designer campaign with \"'`npm run build -- --content path/to/export.json`.\n\nDeveloper mode: Pause → Expedition tools. Live sliders, reset, floor/encounter jumps, invulnerability, supplies, item spawns, checkpoint retry, geometry reveal, state/intent diagnostics, deterministic step and content workshop all use the production model. Edits mark runs modified. In scored Hub play developer tools are read-only and title/restart/signaling are unavailable. The workshop previews tile/entity data, validates, imports/exports and applies edits to a new modified run.\n\nSee docs/CONTROLS.md, docs/RESEARCH.md, docs/rules.md, docs/network.md and docs/presentation.md. Canonical source/feedback/catalogue data is src/report.json; build embeds it and writes root exit-interview.json. PROGRESS.md in the workspace root records milestones. The declared10-minute Hub duration is a pacing target; automated route timings are machine-driven commands, not a human playtime measurement.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
