{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_187","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat src/report.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-06 04:02:57\",\n    \"source\": \"Advanced Dungeons & Dragons: Treasure of Tarmin (1983), Mattel Electronics / APh; program by Tom Loughry.\",\n    \"plan\": \"PLAN.md\",\n    \"genre\": \"RPG; tactical dungeon crawler\",\n    \"perspective\": \"First person; temporary explored automap\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge / World3D v0.89.1 (8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691); three.js 0.182.0; Rapier SIMD compat 0.19.3; AI Hub client 1.4.0 (35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869); browser JavaScript; Bun 1.3.14 bundler.\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"Original procedural pixel-textured stone, lantern-lit volumetric puppets, eye murals and horned machinery, extending native shapes and materials.\",\n    \"animation\": \"Native Humanoid, Blob, Attack, Combo and World3D pen animation; interpolated grid camera and two visible first-person hands; original species and channel effects.\",\n    \"audio\": \"Original code-generated score, effects and short gibberish voices; no audio files. Music defaults to 10%.\",\n    \"online\": \"1–2 shared-world co-op: reliable ordered WebRTC DataChannel with manual offer/answer and disclosed 2–30-second planning (8 seconds stock); AI Hub shared-world protocol including server-only authority. No TURN relay: some NAT/firewall pairs cannot connect. Rendering dependencies are embedded.\",\n    \"testing\": \"Headless normal-rules solo and co-op routes; exact-HTML keyboard, mouse, emulated standard gamepad and phone/tablet touch tests; WebGPU, WebGL 2, WebKit and unsupported-Firefox handling; Web Audio analysis; real two-context WebRTC; official Hub harness plus server, timeout and departure cases. Physical devices, human listening and cross-network NAT unavailable.\",\n    \"research\": \"Read all 17 scanned Mattel manual pages; cross-checked official transcript and publisher history. Retrieved recording excerpt 00:40–01:20 and inspected frames at 00:43, 00:58 and 01:12. Exact original music and timbres unknown; no human listening available. Research links are in docs/RESEARCH.md.\",\n    \"environment\": \"Codex CLI (headless); Linux\",\n    \"model\": \"OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort max\",\n    \"usage\": \"added on import\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\n    \"32 actual implementation issues were caught and fixed; the issue ledger separates them from test-harness mistakes.\",\n    \"0 critical or game-breaking issues were found. Input, menu, co-op access and compatibility defects were fixed before release.\",\n    \"The complete normal-rules replay against the delivered HTML, including 372 acknowledged inputs over real WebRTC with both peers compared after every action.\",\n    \"The initial text search for external script tags matched examples inside embedded engine documentation. Actual HTML parsing and browser request checks were more useful.\"\n  ],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\n    \"World3D uses the same procedural materials, native shapes and articulated rigs throughout a first-person dungeon; the fixed-step lifecycle and menu/rebinding infrastructure provide useful foundations.\",\n    \"Custom input maps need explicit menu directions. Native Shell pause/fade bookkeeping needed integration with a live online world, and the three.js import expected GPUShaderStage constants on Firefox before capability checks could run.\",\n    \"The authoritative deterministic two-strength model, six-slot inventory transactions, authored campaign and cooperative round/transport rules.\",\n    \"Keeping native animation, the first-person camera and menu focus separate from authoritative rules and the independently running network clock.\",\n    \"none; engine and World3D source files remain unmodified. The HTML supplies the standard GPUShaderStage constants when missing, and the game registers native art, poses, moves and instruments.\",\n    \"Ship a self-contained first-person crawler example with renderer-free authority, unsupported-renderer handling and documented live-menu behavior.\"\n  ],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\n    \"The concrete strategic identity, three-floor campaign and explicit treasure-claim ending make the intended game clear.\",\n    \"The plan refers to a different model/context-window size than TASK.md; delivery metadata follows TASK.md.\",\n    \"Keeping complete solo play, native presentation, standalone WebRTC and the Hub lifecycle coherent in one self-contained artifact.\",\n    \"Separate required acceptance checks from optional presentation ideas and supply executable content/session schemas.\"\n  ],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"Advanced Dungeons and Danger\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Two strengths. Six slots. No way back.\",\n    \"description\": \"Enter three lantern-lit vaults where steel and spirit are separate strengths. Read enemy intentions, prepare your six-slot pack, break the Minotaur’s seals and claim the Emberheart, alone or together.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"RPG\",\n    \"source\": \"Advanced Dungeons & Dragons: Treasure of Tarmin (1983)\",\n    \"series\": \"Advanced Dungeons & Dragons\",\n    \"players\": \"1–2\",\n    \"controls\": \"WASD step; Q/E turn; mouse look; F interact; Enter/left click commit; right click guard; Tab pack; X swap; M map; R retreat; Esc menu. Standard gamepad and assisted touch.\",\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"3d\",\n      \"first-person\",\n      \"co-op\",\n      \"fantasy\",\n      \"exploration\",\n      \"boss fights\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_188","type":"agent_message","text":"Built [**Advanced Dungeons and Danger**](./Advanced-Dungeons-and-Danger-Advanced-Dungeons-and-Dragons-Treasure-of-Tarmin-1983-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261106-040257.html), a self-contained HTML game. **249/249 checks passed.**\n\n[Machine-readable report](./exit-interview.json) · [Progress](./PROGRESS.md) · [Test evidence](./Advanced-Dungeons-and-Danger-Advanced-Dungeons-and-Dragons-Treasure-of-Tarmin-1983-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261106-040257/docs/TESTING.md)\n\n**Source Information**\n\n- **Date:** 2026-11-06 04:02:57\n- **Source:** [Advanced Dungeons & Dragons: Treasure of Tarmin (1983)](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/addtarmin.html), Mattel Electronics/APh; programmed by Tom Loughry.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** RPG; tactical dungeon crawler.\n- **Perspective:** First person with an explored automap.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge/World3D 0.89.1, three.js 0.182.0, Rapier 0.19.3, AI Hub client 1.4.0, JavaScript and Bun 1.3.14.\n- **Art Direction:** Original procedural stone textures, lantern-lit volumetric creatures, eye murals and horned machinery.\n- **Animation:** Native Humanoid, Blob, Attack, Combo and World3D systems; interpolated movement and visible hands.\n- **Audio:** Original synthesized music, effects and gibberish voices; no audio files. Music defaults to 10%.\n- **Online:** 1–2-player WebRTC co-op and AI Hub shared-world sessions, including server-only authority. Planning windows support 2–30 seconds; eight is stock. No TURN relay is supplied.\n- **Testing:** Complete normal-rules solo/co-op runs, input emulation, persistence, audio analysis, browser fallbacks and official Hub scenarios. Physical hardware, human listening and cross-network NAT remain unverified.\n- **Research:** Read the [17-page manual](https://mocagh.org/intvaction/addtot-manual.pdf), cross-checked its [transcript](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/instructions/addtarmininst.html) and publisher history, and inspected gameplay frames from 00:40–01:20 of the supplied recording. Exact original music and timbres: unknown.\n- **Environment:** Codex CLI (headless); Linux.\n- **Model:** OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort max.\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n\n1. 32 implementation issues were caught and fixed, recorded separately from test-harness mistakes.\n2. Zero were classified as critical/game-breaking.\n3. Most helpful: the complete normal-rules replay, including 372 acknowledged WebRTC inputs with peer-state comparisons.\n4. Least helpful: text searches for external script tags, which matched examples inside embedded documentation.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n\n1. Native materials, shapes, articulated rigs and menu infrastructure provided a coherent foundation.\n2. Custom input maps needed explicit menu directions; pause/fade behavior and missing WebGPU constants needed integration fixes.\n3. The largest addition was the authoritative two-strength model, inventory transactions, campaign and cooperative rules.\n4. The hardest integration was separating presentation and menu focus from rules and the network clock.\n5. Engine/World3D files remain unmodified. The HTML adds compatibility constants; the game registers native art, poses, moves and instruments.\n6. A self-contained crawler example with renderer-free authority and documented live menus would help.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n\n1. The strategic identity, three-floor campaign and explicit treasure-claim ending made the goal clear.\n2. The plan’s model/context-window reference differs from TASK.md; delivery metadata follows TASK.md.\n3. The hardest part was keeping the complete game and both multiplayer paths coherent in one HTML.\n4. Separate mandatory acceptance checks from optional presentation ideas, and provide executable content/session schemas."}}
